<Section id="styling" title="3. Styling your Shapes">

We'll keep working on the **same grid** from the last section — no new file. Right now every record looks alike; let's colour each one by its **genre** so the chart tells us something at a glance.

The catch: a genre is a piece of text like `"Dance pop"` or `"Hip-hop/R&B"`, and an SVG `fill` needs a colour. We need a way to turn one into the other.

For that we use an **object** as a lookup table — think of it like a **dictionary**: you look up a word (the genre) and get back its definition (the colour). Each line is a **key** (the genre) paired with a **value** (the colour):

```svelte
/// file: Colour map
const genreColors = {
	'Dance pop': '#E75B2B',
	Pop: '#DB1E24',
	'Hip-hop/R&B': '#F9B806',
	'Acoustic/Folk': '#3091B9',
	Other: '#E032A3',
	EDM: '#53DC41'
};
```

To look one up, we put the key in **square brackets**:

```svelte
/// file: Looking up a colour
genreColors['Dance pop']; // "#E75B2B"
genreColors[row.genre]; // the colour for this row's genre
```

We use brackets `[ ]` here (not a dot) because the key comes from the data and some genres contain spaces or slashes — `row.genre` looks up whatever genre that row happens to be.

Now wire it into the drawing. Set the **cell's `fill`** and the **record label's `fill`** to `genreColors[row.genre]`, and the grid colours itself by genre:

<Exercise file="6-drawing-an-entire-dataset.svelte" bleed>
	<DrawingEntireDataset />
</Exercise>

{#snippet sidebar()}

Paste this colour map into your file:

```svelte
/// file: Colour map
const genreColors = {
	'Dance pop': '#E75B2B',
	Pop: '#DB1E24',
	'Hip-hop/R&B': '#F9B806',
	'Acoustic/Folk': '#3091B9',
	Other: '#E032A3',
	EDM: '#53DC41'
};
```

> [!Note] New words
> - **object**: a set of **key → value** pairs, like a dictionary.
> - **key**: what you look up (here, the genre).
> - **value**: what you get back (here, the colour).
> - **lookup**: read a value with square brackets — `genreColors[row.genre]`.

**Colour the cell and the label**

```svelte
/// file: Fills
<rect ... fill={genreColors[row.genre]} />
<circle ... fill={genreColors[row.genre]} />
```

**RESULT**:

